{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-337177" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the first-order linear ODE\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-4y={e}^{4x}.\n$$\n\nWe wish to solve this equation using the method of variation of parameters (VOP).\n\nSelect the correct options below. \n\n$\\,$ According to VOP, we assume a particular solution of the form {{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ Solving for $u,$ we have $u(x)=$ {{field-2}}.\n\n$\\,$ Therefore, the particular solution is ${y}_{p}(x)=$ {{field-3}}." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069549/state.json" "file_sha256" "6dcfa45f563bb44f1449daf44680aec303a99e414e6041db4f03428b467132c5"} {"question" "q-337177" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution ${y}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}^{′}-4{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{4x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nThe method of variation of parameters constructs a particular solution by varying the constant in the homogeneous solution. So, to find a suitable ${y}_{p},$ we set $A=u(x)$ in the complementary solution, as given by the following ansatz:\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=u(x){e}^{4x}\n$$\n\nIntuitively, this form of ${y}_{p}$ tracks the homogeneous solution but with a varying amplitude.\n\nTo find $u,$ we substitute our ansatz into the equation, apply the product rule to the derivative term, and simplify to isolate ${u}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(u{e}^{4x})}^{′}-4(u{e}^{4x}) & ={e}^{4x} \\\\ {u}^{′}{e}^{4x}+4u{e}^{4x}-4u{e}^{4x} & ={e}^{4x} \\\\ {u}^{′}{e}^{4x}+\\cancel{4u{e}^{4x}}-\\cancel{4u{e}^{4x}} & ={e}^{4x} \\\\ {u}^{′}{e}^{4x} & ={e}^{4x} \\\\ {u}^{′} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides with respect to $x$ (omitting the arbitrary constant), we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′} & =1 \\\\ \\int {u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\,\\text{d}x \\\\ u & =x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since ${y}_{p}=u(x){e}^{4x},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p} & =u(x){e}^{4x} \\\\  & =x{e}^{4x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069549/state.json" "file_sha256" "6dcfa45f563bb44f1449daf44680aec303a99e414e6041db4f03428b467132c5"} {"question" "q-337177" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069549/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "776432af58e7225d3f4d76d9ca55f0205cb9d2f90813ff5bd529cc97e7850a5d"} {"question" "q-337177" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/select "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069549/state.json" "file_sha256" "6dcfa45f563bb44f1449daf44680aec303a99e414e6041db4f03428b467132c5"} {"question" "q-337177" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "u(x){e}^{-2x}"] ["math" "u(x){e}^{-4x}"] ["math" "u(x){e}^{2x}"] ["math" "u(x){e}^{4x}"] ["math" "u(x){e}^{6x}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069549/state.json" "file_sha256" "6dcfa45f563bb44f1449daf44680aec303a99e414e6041db4f03428b467132c5"} {"question" "q-337177" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "u(x){e}^{4x}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069549/state.json" "file_sha256" "6dcfa45f563bb44f1449daf44680aec303a99e414e6041db4f03428b467132c5"} {"question" "q-337177" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/select "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069549/state.json" "file_sha256" "6dcfa45f563bb44f1449daf44680aec303a99e414e6041db4f03428b467132c5"} {"question" "q-337177" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "1"] ["math" "x"] ["math" "x{e}^{-4x}"] ["math" "x{e}^{4x}"] ["math" "{e}^{-4x}"] ["math" "{e}^{4x}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069549/state.json" "file_sha256" "6dcfa45f563bb44f1449daf44680aec303a99e414e6041db4f03428b467132c5"} {"question" "q-337177" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "x"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069549/state.json" "file_sha256" "6dcfa45f563bb44f1449daf44680aec303a99e414e6041db4f03428b467132c5"} {"question" "q-337177" "field" "field-3" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/select "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069549/state.json" "file_sha256" "6dcfa45f563bb44f1449daf44680aec303a99e414e6041db4f03428b467132c5"} {"question" "q-337177" "field" "field-3" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "x{e}^{-4x}"] ["math" "x{e}^{4x}"] ["math" "{e}^{-4x}"] ["math" "{e}^{4x}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069549/state.json" "file_sha256" "6dcfa45f563bb44f1449daf44680aec303a99e414e6041db4f03428b467132c5"} {"question" "q-337177" "field" "field-3" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "x{e}^{4x}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069549/state.json" "file_sha256" "6dcfa45f563bb44f1449daf44680aec303a99e414e6041db4f03428b467132c5"} {"question" "q-337376" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the first-order linear ODE\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-3y=5.\n$$\n\nWe wish to solve this equation using the method of variation of parameters (VOP).\n\nSelect the correct options below.\n\n$\\,$ According to VOP, we assume a particular solution of the form {{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ Solving for $u,$ we have $u(x)=$ {{field-2}}.\n\n$\\,$ Therefore, the particular solution is ${y}_{p}(x)=$ {{field-3}}." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069549/state.json" "file_sha256" "6dcfa45f563bb44f1449daf44680aec303a99e414e6041db4f03428b467132c5"} {"question" "q-337376" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution ${y}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}^{′}-3{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{3x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nThe method of variation of parameters constructs a particular solution by varying the constant in the homogeneous solution. So, to find a suitable ${y}_{p},$ we set $A=u(x)$ in the complementary solution, as given by the following ansatz:\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=u(x){e}^{3x}\n$$\n\nIntuitively, this form of ${y}_{p}$ tracks the homogeneous solution but with a varying amplitude.\n\nTo find $u,$ we substitute our ansatz into the equation, apply the product rule to the derivative term, and simplify to isolate ${u}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(u{e}^{3x})}^{′}-3(u{e}^{3x}) & =5 \\\\ {u}^{′}{e}^{3x}+3u{e}^{3x}-3u{e}^{3x} & =5 \\\\ {u}^{′}{e}^{3x}+\\cancel{3u{e}^{3x}}-\\cancel{3u{e}^{3x}} & =5 \\\\ {u}^{′}{e}^{3x} & =5 \\\\ {u}^{′} & =5{e}^{-3x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides with respect to $x$ (omitting the arbitrary constant), we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′} & =5{e}^{-3x} \\\\ \\int {u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int 5{e}^{-3x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ u & =-\\frac{5}{3}{e}^{-3x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since ${y}_{p}=u(x){e}^{3x},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p} & =u(x){e}^{3x} \\\\  & =-\\frac{5}{3}{e}^{-3x}\\cdot {e}^{3x} \\\\  & =-\\frac{5}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069549/state.json" "file_sha256" "6dcfa45f563bb44f1449daf44680aec303a99e414e6041db4f03428b467132c5"} {"question" "q-337376" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069549/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "776432af58e7225d3f4d76d9ca55f0205cb9d2f90813ff5bd529cc97e7850a5d"} {"question" "q-337376" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/select "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069549/state.json" "file_sha256" "6dcfa45f563bb44f1449daf44680aec303a99e414e6041db4f03428b467132c5"} {"question" "q-337376" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "u(x){e}^{-3x}"] ["math" "u(x){e}^{-5x}"] ["math" "u(x){e}^{2x}"] ["math" "u(x){e}^{3x}"] ["math" "u(x){e}^{5x}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069549/state.json" "file_sha256" "6dcfa45f563bb44f1449daf44680aec303a99e414e6041db4f03428b467132c5"} {"question" "q-337376" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "u(x){e}^{3x}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069549/state.json" "file_sha256" "6dcfa45f563bb44f1449daf44680aec303a99e414e6041db4f03428b467132c5"} {"question" "q-337376" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/select "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069549/state.json" "file_sha256" "6dcfa45f563bb44f1449daf44680aec303a99e414e6041db4f03428b467132c5"} {"question" "q-337376" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-\\frac{5}{2}{e}^{-2x}"] ["math" "-\\frac{5}{3}{e}^{-3x}"] ["math" "-\\frac{5}{3}{e}^{3x}"] ["math" "-\\frac{5}{3}{e}^{5x}"] ["math" "\\frac{5}{3}{e}^{-3x}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069549/state.json" "file_sha256" "6dcfa45f563bb44f1449daf44680aec303a99e414e6041db4f03428b467132c5"} {"question" "q-337376" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-\\frac{5}{3}{e}^{-3x}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069549/state.json" "file_sha256" "6dcfa45f563bb44f1449daf44680aec303a99e414e6041db4f03428b467132c5"} {"question" "q-337376" "field" "field-3" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/select "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069549/state.json" "file_sha256" "6dcfa45f563bb44f1449daf44680aec303a99e414e6041db4f03428b467132c5"} {"question" "q-337376" "field" "field-3" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-\\frac{5}{3}"] ["math" "-\\frac{5}{3}{e}^{-3x}"] ["math" "-\\frac{5}{3}{e}^{3x}"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{3}"] ["math" "\\frac{5}{3}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069549/state.json" "file_sha256" "6dcfa45f563bb44f1449daf44680aec303a99e414e6041db4f03428b467132c5"} {"question" "q-337376" "field" "field-3" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-\\frac{5}{3}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069549/state.json" "file_sha256" "6dcfa45f563bb44f1449daf44680aec303a99e414e6041db4f03428b467132c5"} {"question" "q-337399" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the first-order linear ODE\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+2y=\\operatorname{sinh}⁡3x.\n$$\n\nWe wish to solve this equation using the method of variation of parameters (VOP).\n\nSelect the correct options below.\n\n$\\,$ According to VOP, we assume a particular solution of the form {{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ Solving for $u,$ we have $u(x)=$ {{field-2}}.\n\n$\\,$ Therefore, the particular solution is ${y}_{p}(x)=$ {{field-3}}." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069549/state.json" "file_sha256" "6dcfa45f563bb44f1449daf44680aec303a99e414e6041db4f03428b467132c5"} {"question" "q-337399" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution ${y}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}^{′}+2{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{-2x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nThe method of variation of parameters constructs a particular solution by varying the constant in the homogeneous solution. So, to find a suitable ${y}_{p},$ we set $A=u(x)$ in the complementary solution, as given by the following ansatz:\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=u(x){e}^{-2x}\n$$\n\nIntuitively, this form of ${y}_{p}$ tracks the homogeneous solution but with a varying amplitude.\n\nTo find $u,$ we substitute our ansatz into the equation, apply the product rule to the derivative term, and simplify to isolate ${u}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(u{e}^{-2x})}^{′}+2(u{e}^{-2x}) & =\\operatorname{sinh}⁡3x \\\\ {u}^{′}{e}^{-2x}-2u{e}^{-2x}+2u{e}^{-2x} & =\\operatorname{sinh}⁡3x \\\\ {u}^{′}{e}^{-2x}-\\cancel{2u{e}^{-2x}}+\\cancel{2u{e}^{-2x}} & =\\operatorname{sinh}⁡3x \\\\ {u}^{′}{e}^{-2x} & =\\operatorname{sinh}⁡3x \\\\ {u}^{′} & ={e}^{2x}\\operatorname{sinh}⁡3x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides with respect to $x$ (omitting the arbitrary constant), we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′} & ={e}^{2x}\\operatorname{sinh}⁡3x \\\\ \\int {u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int {e}^{2x}\\operatorname{sinh}⁡3x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ u & =\\int {e}^{2x}\\operatorname{sinh}⁡3x\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since ${y}_{p}=u(x){e}^{-2x},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p} & =u(x){e}^{-2x} \\\\  & =\\int {e}^{2x}\\operatorname{sinh}⁡3x\\,\\text{d}x\\cdot {e}^{-2x} \\\\  & ={e}^{-2x}\\int {e}^{2x}\\operatorname{sinh}⁡3x\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069549/state.json" "file_sha256" "6dcfa45f563bb44f1449daf44680aec303a99e414e6041db4f03428b467132c5"} {"question" "q-337399" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academ